    "content": "In September 2023, golfer Gary Woodland underwent successful surgery to remove a lesion (tumor) from his brain that was causing severe fear and anxiety symptoms. Although part of the lesion remains to protect his motor functions, he returned to the PGA Tour in 2024, subsequently battling PTSD before winning the 2026 Houston Open. Key Details About Gary Woodland's Brain Surgery & Recovery: The Condition: A lesion was discovered on his brain that caused extreme anxiety, terror, and, in some cases, symptoms of fear and anxiety. The Procedure: Surgeons at the Mayo Clinic removed the majority of the lesion on Sept. 19, 2023, which involved creating a hole in his skull. The Aftermath: While the surgery removed the immediate neurological threat, Yahoo Sports notes that Woodland developed PTSD, experiencing severe anxiety and fear of dying. Return to Play: He made his return to the PGA Tour in January 2024 at the Sony Open, despite still facing significant mental health challenges, The Killeen Daily Herald says. Recovery and Success: He won the 2026 Houston Open, his first victory since the procedure, marking an emotional comeback, ESPN reports. Woodland has been vocal about his journey to raise awareness for mental health and the lasting effects of neurological trauma.",
